From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin Walsh <contact.me@thejustinwalsh.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 13:20:48 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] cuda: QSA prompt attention on tensor cores, one block
 per (query, KV head)

Port of the idea of Strata's D-1 (Niko1221/Strata dce4598, "the prompt path's QSA
attention on tensor cores", src/kernels/cuda/qsa_prompt_attn.cu, MIT; notice in
engines/patches/llama-upstream-flash/LICENSE.strata): one block per (query, KV head)
walks that query's OWN selected cells in chunks of 32 with an online softmax -- no
union of the neighbouring queries' selections, no split-K scratch, no merge kernel --
and q.k and p.v are f16 MMAs with FP32 accumulation over the gqa query heads of the
KV head (padded to the MMA's 16 rows).

Why: a Flash-Next QSA layer (256/256, 2 KV heads, gqa 12, indexer top-k 2,048) sends a
prompt batch to ggml's wide-tile FLASH_ATTN_EXT: below 2 x 8 x n_kv_max = ~32.9K cells
of cache (fattn.cu shall_use_sparse: K->ne[1] >= 2 x ncols1 x n_kv_max) that is the
DENSE kernel over every cell with the selection as a mask; above, a gather of the UNION
of 8 queries' selections per tile. Both read or compute far more than the 2,051 cells a
query attends to.

What is Strata's and what is ours (also in the file header): Strata's are the work
split (block per (query, KV head), 4 warps, scores by cell, p.v by dimension), the
chunk loop and its barriers, the online softmax and the exact-zero treatment of masked
cells, and the int8 -> half mantissa trick. Ours: it reads ggml's own data -- the
selection is the FLASH_ATTN_EXT mask (finite = selected, its value added), compacted to
one list per query by the existing flash_attn_mask_to_sparse_indices<1>; the cache is
ggml's f16 / q8_0 / q4_0 rows, dequantized while gathered into shared memory to the
same f16 values the existing conversion makes (x * d rounded once; checked bit for bit
against it offline). Not ported: Strata's int8-codes-with-FP32-scales and the hi/lo
splits of q and p (two MMAs for FP32-level accuracy): ggml's FA is f16 q, k, v and p
with FP32 accumulation and that is the numerics this build ships. K and V fragments
come from ldmatrix (.trans for V).

Switch: GGML_CUDA_QSA_PROMPT_ATTN=1 (plain kernel) or =2 (the next chunk's raw q8_0 /
q4_0 words loaded into registers while the current chunk computes; f16 takes the plain
one). Off by default: with it unset the dispatch returns at once and every path is the
one before. GGML_CUDA_QSA_PROMPT_ATTN_POISON=1 (tests only) fills the shared tiles with
NaN first. Used only when: Ampere or later; 256/256; f16 / q8_0 / q4_0 K and V of one
type; one sequence; a mask, no sinks, no ALiBi or softcap; the op's n_kv_max hint set;
gqa <= 16; at least two blocks' worth of (query, KV head) pairs per SM (a decode or an
MTP verify window keeps the decode kernels); and K->ne[1] >= max(4096, 2 x n_kv_max)
(the sparse path's own rule).

Tests: test-backend-ops FLASH_ATTN_EXT gains the model's prompt batches (f16, q8_0,
q4_0; the permuted cache view; 64 / 512 / 513 / 1024 queries at 5,120 / 16,896 cells,
512 at 35,840; top-k 2,051 and 2,048; gqa 12, 16, 6), all against the CPU backend's
result; they run with the switch off (the existing paths) and on (1, 2, poisoned).
NOT RUN ON A GPU when this was written (no GPU was available): compiled for sm_86 and
sm_120a without spills (144 / 165 / 181 registers, 36,352 bytes of shared memory), the
fragment and softmax dataflow checked against a numpy reference by emulating the
kernel thread by thread (nmse 6e-8), the dequantization checked bit for bit against
x * d rounded to f16 (q8_0 and q4_0, 300 random rows). Speed is NOT measured.
